When he wrote and directed the short film Life Sentence , Panthaki realized that he loved those behind-the-scenes roles. &#8220;That film was about something that was really personal to me,&#8221; Panthaki tells THR in Tallinn. &#8220;And when it came to getting made, I didn't want anyone else to make it. I've lost someone close to me, so this was a story that I had to tell. It was a five-day shoot on a short film with a minuscule budget, and I realized when I stepped on set that this, in some ways, comes more naturally to me than acting. So I realized it was a massive calling.&#8221;

In Starland is also very close to Panthaki's heart, comparing it to a child. &#8220;I realized that the first feature film, which is why it's taken so many years, had to be something very personal, something that I wanted to say about the world,&#8221; he shares.

