Maclin lauds Panthaki for his conviction and vision. &#8220;Ray sees the world in his own way, and you can feel that in this film,&#8221; the star tells THR . &#8220;He knows what he wants, but he's also open to what you bring to it. He gives you room to be yourself, to try things, to find something you maybe didn't even know was there. And that's where the magic happens.&#8221;

In Starland premieres at a time when more of its cast members have broken out. &#8220;Miss Benny is, of course, in Club Kid ,&#8221; notes Panthaki. &#8220;Then you got Bella, who is everywhere, and Tai'mua, this amazing Hawaiian actor, was in an Oscar-nominated short [ Jane Austen's Period Drama ] playing a lead role. Everyone's having their moment.&#8221;

While In Starland follows a group of young creators obsessed with views, likes and followers, &#8220;I didn't want to know about the cast's follower count,&#8221; the director recalls. &#8220;It was only about whether that person would earn the role in the room, and they did.&#8221;

And he needed actors happy to improvise. &#8220;That is how I like to do things,&#8221; the writer-director explains. &#8220;Once I have a script, I like to find truth and play around and naturalize things as much as possible. So I needed actors who were able to do that. I needed actors that I work very much on instinct, so I could turn up on a day and say, &#8216;This doesn't feel right to me today. We're going to do something completely different.' That happened a number of times on set.&#8221;

